---
import '../styles/shared.css';
import '../styles/pages/blog-811-locates.css';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const meta = {
  title: "Call 811 Before You Dig | Spokane Stump Grinder Blog",
  description: "Why calling 811 before a stump grinding or removal job matters, what's typically buried in a yard, and how the locate process works.",
  active: "blog.html",
};
---
<Base {...meta}>
<div class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Blog</p>
    <h1>Call 811 before you dig: what it means for your stump job</h1>
    <p>Grinding a stump — and especially pulling the root ball for a full removal — goes deeper into the yard than almost any other project. Here's what 811 actually covers, and what it doesn't.</p>
    <p class="post-meta">August 2026</p>
  </div>
</div>

<section>
  <div class="wrap article">

    <h2>What 811 actually is</h2>
    <p>811 is the national "call before you dig" number. Dialing it (or filing a request online) connects you to your state's one-call center — Washington 811 or Idaho 811, depending on where the job is — which notifies the utility companies with lines near your address. It's a free service; there's no charge to request a locate.</p>

    <h2>Why it matters more for stump work than most yard projects</h2>
    <p>A mower or an edger never leaves the top inch or two of soil. Grinding a stump goes well below that, and digging out a root ball for a full removal goes deeper still — right into the zone where sprinkler lines, low-voltage lighting, invisible fence wire, and utility lines often sit. Most of that is invisible until someone hits it.</p>

    <h2>What's usually down there</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="chip-card">
        <strong>Gas, electric, water &amp; sewer</strong>
        <span>Utility-owned — covered by an 811 locate</span>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-card">
        <strong>Cable &amp; internet</strong>
        <span>Utility-owned — covered by an 811 locate</span>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-card">
        <strong>Sprinkler / irrigation lines</strong>
        <span>Usually private — often not covered</span>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-card">
        <strong>Low-voltage landscape lighting</strong>
        <span>Usually private — often not covered</span>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-card">
        <strong>Invisible pet fence wire</strong>
        <span>Usually private — often not covered</span>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-card">
        <strong>Septic lines &amp; tanks</strong>
        <span>Usually private — often not covered</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The important nuance:</strong> 811 locates mark utility-owned lines up to the meter or connection point. Anything on your side of that — your own sprinkler system, landscape lighting, or invisible fence — is usually not covered by the free locate. That part relies on what you know about your own yard.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How the process works</h2>
    <p>In broad strokes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A locate request goes in with the address and a description of the work area.</li>
      <li>Utilities are notified and typically have a couple of business days to respond by marking their lines — flags or spray paint, color-coded by utility type (the industry-standard colors: red for electric, yellow for gas, orange for communications, blue for water, green for sewer).</li>
      <li>Once everything's marked, or the ticket clears with no conflicts nearby, the locate is good for a set window before it expires and needs to be refreshed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted-note">Exact wait times and ticket validity windows are set by each state's one-call center, not by us — if timing matters for your project, confirm current numbers with Washington 811 or Idaho 811 directly.</p>

    <h2>Who requests the locate</h2>
    <p>We do. When you book a job with us, we submit the 811 request as part of scheduling — you don't need to call it in yourself.</p>

    <h2>What you can do to help</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Flag or mark anything you know is private — sprinkler heads, low-voltage wire, invisible fence — since the utility locate won't catch those.</li>
      <li>Keep the marked area accessible until the work is done: no parking over flags, no fresh mulch or mowing over paint marks.</li>
      <li>Mention any known buried lines in the notes box when you <a href="service-request.html">request service</a> — it's the same thing we ask about on that form.</li>
    </ul>

    <a class="back-link" href="blog.html">&larr; Back to all posts</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cta">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Getting a stump ground or removed?</h2>
    <p>Tell us about the job and anything you know is buried nearby — we'll take it from there.</p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="service-request.html">Request Service</a>
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href="booking.html">Book an OSC</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
